-- title:
--   Component group of J_H(M) at p ∥ M from annulus depths
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $p$ is a prime and $M$ a positive level with $p \mid M$ (`hpM`) and $p^2 \nmid M$ (`hpM2`), and $H \le (\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$ is a subgroup containing every unit whose reduction modulo $M/p$ is trivial (`hHp`). Further, $A$ is a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ for which $p$ is a non-unit of $A$ (`A.LiesOverPrime p`), whose residue field $\kappa$ has characteristic $p$ and is algebraically closed. Write $F_M$ for `xHFunctionFieldBar M H`, the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-subfield of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}((q))$ generated by the $q$-expansions of the function field of $X_H(M)$, $F_{M/p}$ for the analogous field at level $M/p$ with the group `infSubgroup p M H hpM` (the image of $H$ in $(\mathbb{Z}/(M/p))^\times$), and $\bar F =$ `Fbar p M H hpM κ` for the characteristic-$p$ $q$-expansion function field at the congruence group `ΓN p M H hpM`. Places, divisors, $\operatorname{ord}$, $\operatorname{Pic}^0$ and the degree-zero subgroup are those of the project's function-field formalism, and $J_H(M) = \operatorname{Pic}^0(F_M)$.
--
--   The remaining data are: an $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-algebra automorphism $\theta$ of $F_M$; an integral $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-algebra map $\alpha : F_{M/p} \to F_M$ (`hα`) such that $\beta := \theta \circ \alpha$ is integral as well (`hβ`), with $\alpha$ acting as the identity on $q$-expansions (`hα_coe`) and $\beta$ acting by $q \mapsto q^p$, i.e. by `qExpand _ p`, on $q$-expansions (`hβ_coe`); a unit $pb$ of $\mathbb{Z}/(M/p)$ whose underlying residue is $p$ (`hpb`); a self-map $\delta$ of the places of $\bar F$ given (`hδ`) by the action of the semilinear automorphism attached, via `SemilinearAut.ofAlgAut`, to the mod-$p$ diamond automorphism `diamondActionModL` of $pb$ at level $M/p$; a finite set $SS$ of pairs of places of $\bar F$ whose members are exactly the pairs $s$ with $s_2$ a supersingular place and $s_1 = \Phi(s_2)$, where $\Phi =$ `qExpFrobeniusPlaceModL` (`hSS`); a place specialisation $Psp$ of type `JHPlaceSpecialization p M H hpM A`; and a prolongation datum $Rpd$ for $Psp$ and $\theta$, consisting of two regular prolongations $R_1, R_2$ of $A$ from $F_M$ to $\bar F$ compatible with $\theta$. Throughout, $\mathrm{reduceFst}\,W = Psp.\mathrm{sp}(W|_\alpha)$, $\mathrm{reduceSnd}\,W = \delta(Psp.\mathrm{sp}(W|_\beta))$, a place $W$ of $F_M$ is strict of the first kind when $\delta(\Phi(\mathrm{reduceFst}\,W)) = \mathrm{reduceSnd}\,W$ and $\mathrm{reduceFst}\,W$ is not $\delta$-fixed, strict of the second kind when $\mathrm{reduceFst}\,W = \Phi(\mathrm{reduceSnd}\,W)$ and $\mathrm{reduceSnd}\,W$ is not $\delta$-fixed (a place $v$ of $\bar F$ being $\delta$-fixed, `Fixed`, when $\Phi(\delta(\Phi v)) = v$), $\mathrm{fstDiv}$ and $\mathrm{sndDiv}$ are the restrictions of a divisor to the places of the first, resp. second, kind, and a divisor is good (`IsGoodDiv`) when every place in its support is strict of one of the two kinds.
--
--   The hypotheses fall into the following groups.
--
--   (i) The law block: `hFix` (every supersingular place $y$ of $\bar F$, and also $\Phi(y)$, is $\delta$-fixed); `hTD`, the type dichotomy (for every place $W$ of $F_M$, either $\mathrm{reduceFst}\,W = \Phi(\mathrm{reduceSnd}\,W)$ or $\delta(\Phi(\mathrm{reduceFst}\,W)) = \mathrm{reduceSnd}\,W$); `hmodel`, asserting the four laws `DivisorLawFst`, `DivisorLawSnd`, `CuspLawInfty`, `CuspLawZero` of $Rpd$ (push-forward of the first-kind, resp. second-kind, part of a principal divisor along $\mathrm{reduceFst}$, resp. $\mathrm{reduceSnd}$, computes the order of the corresponding residue at non-$\delta$-fixed places, and the analogous statements for the cuspidal parts at infinity-side, resp. zero-side, places); `hO`, the order law at $\delta$-fixed affine places, where `IsAffinePlace` means that the element of $\bar F$ with $q$-expansion `jqModC κ` has a value at the place; `hreg`, the two-part regularity law; `hnv`, the node value law; and `hθgal`, the commutation of $\theta$ with the arithmetic Galois action of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}} \simeq_{\mathbb{Q}} \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ on $F_M$.
--
--   (ii) Finiteness: `hFixFin`, the set of $\delta$-fixed places of $\bar F$ is finite.
--
--   (iii) The two-pole hypotheses `hLFst` and `hLSnd`, one for each kind (each a single implication with the clauses summarised here): given two distinct places $Q \ne Q'$ of $F_M$, both strict of the first (resp. second) kind, with the same reduction $\mathrm{reduceFst}$ (resp. $\mathrm{reduceSnd}$), that reduction being an affine place, a natural number $n$ nonzero in $\kappa$, and an element $g$ of the integers of $R_1$ (resp. $R_2$) with nonzero residue such that $\operatorname{ord}_Q g = -n$, $\operatorname{ord}_{Q'} g = n$ and $\operatorname{ord}_W g = 0$ for every further place $W$ of the same kind with the same reduction, and a presentation $g = 1 + \iota(e)\varepsilon$ with $e \in A$ and $\varepsilon$ in the integers of $R_1$ (resp. $R_2$) with nonzero residue, the conclusion is $\operatorname{ord}(\text{residue of }\varepsilon) \ge -1$ at that reduction place.
--
--   (iv) The unit hypothesis `hUnit`: there are $u_1, u_2 \in F_M$ and divisors $D_1, D_2$ which are the divisors of $u_1$ and $u_2$, such that $u_1$ and $u_1^{-1}$ lie in the integers of $R_1$ with nonzero residue for $u_1$, the push-forward of $\mathrm{fstDiv}\,D_1$ along $\mathrm{reduceFst}$ computes the order of the $R_1$-residue of $u_1$ at every non-$\delta$-fixed place, the corresponding cusp law holds for $D_1$ at infinity-side places, and every nonzero $f \in F_M$ admits $m \ne 0$ and $j \in \mathbb{Z}$ with $f^m u_1^{\,j}$ in the integers of $R_2$ with nonzero residue; together with the mirror clauses for $u_2$, $R_2$, $\mathrm{sndDiv}$, $\mathrm{reduceSnd}$, the zero-side cusp law, and the integers of $R_1$.
--
--   (v) The cusp and orientation hypotheses: `hcusp` (every non-affine place $w$ of $\bar F$ is $\mathrm{reduceFst}$ of some infinity-side place and $\mathrm{reduceSnd}$ of some zero-side place); `horientInf` ($\delta(\Phi(\mathrm{reduceFst}\,C)) = \mathrm{reduceSnd}\,C$ for $C$ infinity-side) and `horient0` ($\mathrm{reduceFst}\,C = \Phi(\mathrm{reduceSnd}\,C)$ for $C$ zero-side).
--
--   (vi) Widths and annuli: a function $e : SS \to \mathbb{N}$ with $e(s) > 0$ for all $s$ (`he`), and `hAnn`, which for every node $s \in SS$ provides an annulus $An$ over $A$ in $F_M$ — a set of rational places, a parameter and a modulus in the maximal ideal of $A$, subject to the axioms of [`AlgebraicCurve.Annulus`](def/AlgebraicCurve_SemistableCharts.html#L86) — satisfying seven clauses: its domain consists exactly of the places $W$ with $\mathrm{reduceFst}\,W = s_1$ that are strict of neither kind; its modulus is $p^{e(s)}$ times a unit of $A$; its parameter is fixed by the arithmetic Galois action of the inertia subgroup of $A$ over $\mathbb{Q}$; $(\text{modulus})^{-1}\cdot\text{param}$ lies in the integers of $R_1$; the parameter lies in the integers of $R_2$ with nonzero residue; the parameter is attached to the second Gauss prolongation at $s_2$, in the sense that its $R_2$-residue has order $1$ at $s_2$ and that for every $f$ in the integers of $R_2$ with nonzero residue and vanishing order along the whole domain, the quantity $\mathrm{ev}_P(f)\cdot \mathrm{ev}_P(\text{param})^{-\operatorname{ord}_{s_2}(\text{residue of } f)}$ is a unit of $A$ at every $P$ of the domain; and the mirror clause attaching $(\text{modulus})\cdot\text{param}^{-1}$ to the first Gauss prolongation at $s_1$.
--
--   (vii) The slope hypothesis `hVSlope`: for every family $s \mapsto An(s)$ of annuli satisfying, for each $s$, the same seven clauses as in `hAnn`, and every $k \in \mathbb{N}$ divisible by all $e(s)$, there exist $f \ne 0$ in $F_M$ and $c \in \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $c \cdot f$ in the integers of $R_1$ having nonzero residue, such that the divisor of $f$ is good, $\operatorname{ord}_V f = 0$ for every place $V$ whose $\mathrm{reduceFst}$ is $\delta$-fixed and is not the first component of a node of $SS$, the residue of $c\cdot f$ has order $0$ at every such $\delta$-fixed non-node place of $\bar F$, and for every $s$ there is $a \ne 0$ in $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $\operatorname{ord}_P f = 0$ and $\mathrm{ev}_P(f)\cdot a\cdot \mathrm{ev}_P(\text{param of } An(s))^{-(k/e(s))}$ a unit of $A$ for all $P$ in the domain of $An(s)$.
--
--   (viii) Reading hypotheses at $\delta$-fixed non-node places: `hFixReadFst` and `hFixReadSnd` (a unit of $R_1$, resp. $R_2$, with nonzero residue having vanishing order at every place above such a fibre place has residue of order $0$ there), and `hFixRegFst`, `hFixRegSnd` (the same with 'no pole', $0 \le \operatorname{ord}$, in place of 'order $0$', at $\delta$-fixed affine non-node fibre places).
--
--   Under these hypotheses there exist a depth function $\mathrm{depth}$ from the places of $F_M$ to $\mathbb{N}$ and a homomorphism of additive groups $\mathrm{comp}$ from the inertia invariants of $J_H(M)$ — the classes killed by the action of the inertia subgroup of $A$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ — to the component group $\Phi(e) = \operatorname{Hom}_{\mathbb{Z}}(L, \mathbb{Z})/\operatorname{im}(\mathrm{gramMap}\,e)$, where $L$ is the character lattice, the kernel of the sum of coordinates on $SS \to \mathbb{Z}$, such that the following six assertions hold.
--
--   First, the depth–component law `DepthCompLaw` holds for $(\alpha, \beta, \delta, SS, e, \mathrm{depth}, \mathrm{comp})$: for every degree-zero divisor $D$ whose class lies in the inertia invariants, such that every place $V$ in the support of $D$ is fixed by the arithmetic Galois action of the inertia subgroup of $A$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ and is strict of the first kind, or strict of the second kind, or satisfies $\mathrm{reduceFst}\,V = s_1$ for some $s \in SS$, and for every $s_0 \in SS$,
--   $$\mathrm{comp}([D]) = \overline{\;\mathrm{depthDual}(D) + \deg(\mathrm{sndDiv}\,D)\cdot\bigl(e(s_0)\,\mathrm{crossingCoord}(s_0)\bigr)\;},$$
--   where $\mathrm{depthDual}(D) = \sum_{s \in SS} \mathrm{depthDiv}(\mathrm{depth}, D, s_1)\cdot \mathrm{crossingCoord}(s)$, $\mathrm{crossingCoord}(s)$ is the $s$-th coordinate functional restricted to $L$, and the bar denotes the image under `componentGroupProj e`.
--
--   Second, for every node $s \in SS$ and every annulus $An$ over $A$ in $F_M$ satisfying the seven clauses of the annulus block at $s$ listed in (vi), the law `AnnulusDepthLawInf` holds for $An$ and $\mathrm{depth}$: for every place $V$ of $F_M$ with $\mathrm{reduceFst}\,V = s_1$ that is fixed by the inertia action,
--   $$v_A\bigl(\mathrm{ev}_V(\iota(\text{modulus of } An)\cdot(\text{param of } An)^{-1})\bigr) \;=\; v_A(p)^{\mathrm{depth}(V)} .$$
--
--   Third, the second-kind degree law: for every degree-zero divisor $D$ whose class lies in the inertia invariants and which is a good divisor, and for every $s_0 \in SS$,
--   $$\mathrm{comp}([D]) = \deg(\mathrm{sndDiv}\,D)\cdot \mathrm{componentGroupProj}\bigl(e(s_0)\,\mathrm{crossingCoord}(s_0)\bigr),$$
--   the functional being written in the Lean as $(e(s_0) : \mathbb{Z})$ times the composite of the projection to the $s_0$-th coordinate with the inclusion of the character lattice.
--
--   Fourth, $\mathrm{comp}$ is surjective.
--
--   Fifth, every class in the kernel has a good representative: if $\mathrm{comp}(x) = 0$ for $x$ in the inertia invariants, then there is a degree-zero divisor $D$ on $F_M$ which is a good divisor and whose class $[D]$ equals $x$ in $J_H(M)$.
--
--   Sixth, there is a divisor $G$ on $F_M$ which is principal, is a good divisor, and satisfies
--   $$\deg(\mathrm{fstDiv}\,G) = \sum_{s \in SS} \frac{\operatorname{lcm}_{s'}(e)}{e(s)}, \qquad \deg(\mathrm{sndDiv}\,G) = -\sum_{s \in SS} \frac{\operatorname{lcm}_{s'}(e)}{e(s)} .$$
--
--   This is the Raynaud-style description, in the formalism of places and prolongations used here, of the component group of the Jacobian $J_H(M)$ at a prime $p$ exactly dividing the level: the depths of the inertia-fixed places on the node annuli together determine a surjection from the inertia invariants of $J_H(M)$ onto the component group $\Phi(e)$ attached to the supersingular node set $SS$ and the widths $e$, with a good representative for each kernel class and a principal good divisor of bidegree $(m(e), -m(e))$. It is used by [`ModularCurve.JHPlaceSpecialization.exists_comp_sndDegLaw_surjective_repOfKer_principalGood_of_isModel_of_coe_of_unit_of_cusp_of_attachedAnnulus_of_slope_of_fixReg`](thm.html#ModularCurve.JHPlaceSpecialization.exists_comp_sndDegLaw_surjective_repOfKer_principalGood_of_isModel_of_coe_of_unit_of_cusp_of_attachedAnnulus_of_slope_of_fixReg), which repackages the conclusion without the depth function, in the chain leading to the analysis of $J_H(M)$ in characteristic $p$ used for level lowering at $p$.
